WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.963
"Classical population-learning methods such as
Policy Space Response Oracles grow a set

00:00:04.043 --> 00:00:08.005
of strategies by repeatedly training a new policy
to best-respond to a mixture over the

00:00:08.085 --> 00:00:12.048
existing ones.

00:00:12.128 --> 00:00:16.091
In toy normal-form games this works cleanly because
best-responses can be solved exactly.

00:00:16.171 --> 00:00:20.134
But real-world games are temporal and partially observed,

00:00:20.214 --> 00:00:24.176
so best-responses can only be approximated with
expensive deep reinforcement learning.

00:00:24.256 --> 00:00:28.219
This creates two failures: under a finite budget
you cannot tell a truly converged best-response

00:00:28.299 --> 00:00:32.262
from one stuck at a local plateau,

00:00:32.342 --> 00:00:36.305
so iterations get truncated prematurely and pollute
the population with weak policies;

00:00:36.385 --> 00:00:40.347
and every new policy relearns basic skills from scratch,

00:00:40.427 --> 00:00:44.390
which becomes intractable as opponents grow stronger."
